    "q-124826": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124826",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-2,1,0)$, $Y(-1,0,4)$, and $Z(-1,2,-2).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree.",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-2,1,0⟩,\\,y=⟨-1,0,4⟩,\\,z=⟨-1,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156781/assets/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩-⟨-1,2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4⟩-⟨-1,2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{40}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{240}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{4\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{15}})={25}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
