      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find $∠PQR$ given the points $P(-3,0,-1)$, $Q(-1,1,0)$, and $R(1,2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-3,0,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨-1,1,0⟩,\\,r=⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠PQR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156781/assets/209d43a5aab5d57fbd4cd7b421d41f0fa85bc58cfdd36251a8acf392b0ba7312.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{QP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{QR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QP} & =p-q \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,-1⟩-⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{QR} & =r-q \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩-⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{QP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{QR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{QP}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{QR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{QP}||\\overset{\\to }{QR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
