    "q-77167": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77167",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the points $A(2t,4,3),$ $B(5t-1,4,3)$ and $C(2t+4,t+5,3).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,b=⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156781/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨5t-1,\\,4,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+4,\\,t+5,\\,3⟩-⟨2t,\\,4,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,t+1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=8,$ we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =8 \\\\ (3t-1)\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (t+1)+0\\cdot 0 & =8 \\\\ 4(3t-1)+0+0 & =8 \\\\ 12t-4 & =8 \\\\ 12t & =12 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})≈{26.57}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
